<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Alchon Huns</h1><p class="st-meta">370 CE – 570 CE (approx.) · capital Kabul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hunnic people who ruled Kabulistan and Gandhara and invaded northern India, remembered in Indian tradition for the destructive kings Toramana and Mihirakula.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Alchon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> emerged in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Bactria</a> in the late 4th century, probably among the Hunnic groups that followed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chionites">Chionites</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kidarite-kingdom">Kidarites</a>. They first issued coins copying the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>, then moved south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and later replacing the Kidarites in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gandhara">Gandhara</a> in the mid-5th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Known mainly from coins with their distinctive elongated skulls and the Bactrian word 'alchono', and from Indian inscriptions, they ruled as warrior kings over a settled population. Around 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toramana">Toramana</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, reaching central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, as recorded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eran">Eran</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakala">Sakala</a> and was remembered by Buddhist writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> as a persecutor who destroyed monasteries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> was defeated around 528 by the Indian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasodharman">Yasodharman</a> and possibly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a>, and Alchon power retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gandhara">Gandhara</a>. By the mid-6th century their rule faded, and they were succeeded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> region by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nezak-huns">Nezak Huns</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/alchon-huns.jpg" alt="Alchon Huns" width="320" height="398"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alchon_Khingila_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>370 CE – 570 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khingila, Toramana, Mihirakula</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shaivism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bactrian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alkhan, Alchono</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=470&amp;focus=alchon-huns" aria-label="Alchon Huns on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6437 927 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Alchon Huns lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6757 1183L6679 1109M6757 1183L7362 1550"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6679 1109h0M7362 1550h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6757 1183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kabul. <a href="/atlas?year=470&amp;focus=alchon-huns">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
